When API behavior changes:

1. verify Controller behavior and relevant integration tests;
2. verify response metadata;
3. start the API when the change affects startup/composition;
4. open the Development Swagger UI and confirm it loads;
5. exercise representative endpoints through Swagger when that is a meaningful
   behavioral check;
6. preserve newly verified behavior in maintained tests (`verify` step 4).

```json
// BAD - secrets committed to source control
{
  "Jwt": { "Key": "super-secret-key" },
  "Database": { "ConnectionString": "Server=prod;Password=secret" }
}

// GOOD - appsettings.json holds defaults and structure only
{
  "Jwt": { "Key": "", "Issuer": "myapp", "Audience": "myapp" },
  "Database": { "ConnectionString": "" }
}
// Secrets come from user-secrets (development) or environment
// variables / a managed secret store (deployed environments).
```

Review the final changed-file set and diff before choosing any checks. Confirm:

- changes match the requested scope and no unrelated refactoring slipped in;
- no generated or build artifacts were added accidentally;
- no secrets or environment-specific credentials were introduced;
- temporary diagnostics and probes are removed only after step 4 preserved any
  behavior they uniquely verified.

Azure Key Vault is one possible managed secret provider; use the provider that
matches the hosting environment and add Azure-specific dependencies only when
the project actually uses Azure.

```json
// BAD - secrets committed to source control
{
  "Jwt": { "Key": "super-secret-key" },
  "Database": { "ConnectionString": "Server=prod;Password=secret" }
}

// GOOD - appsettings.json holds defaults and structure only
{
